  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Complicated forecast for next week may lead to decreasing\nrainfall chances. \n\nOverall, a quiet period is forecast through the remainder of the\nweekend and into early next week as high pressure sets up and\nremains in influence over the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast \nthrough at least Wednesday. While a disheveled shortwave is\nexpected to move through the region late Sunday into Monday,\nmuch of the region will continue to remain dry thanks to the\naforementioned surface high pressure system advecting dry air\ninto the region ahead of this upper level shortwave trough.\nNevertheless, some light showers can't be ruled out in extreme\nSW Virginia and portions of SE West Virginia in upslope\nlocations late Sunday into Monday morning. The area looks to \ncompletely dry out once again on Tuesday and most of Wednesday\nacross the region as high pressure slides east off the Mid-\nAtlantic states coastline. \n\nBy late Wednesday, the aforementioned disheveled shortwave \ntrough looks to potentially develop a subtropical low off the \ncoast of Florida in a very moist environment. The placement and \ntiming of this low is quite variable at this time. At the same \ntime, much more amplified upper level troughs are expected to \nslide east into the central Conus. Forecast models had been \nconsistent in deepening an upper level trough and digging it \nsouth into the northern Gulf states mid to late week; however, \nguidance no longer digs this trough as far south, which looks to\nreally limit the amount of moisture that was expected to be \npulled north into the region. Along with this change in upper \nlevel dynamics, the aforementioned low off the Florida \ncoastline looks to possibly prevent moisture from being pulled \nnorth as well ahead of a cold front associated with the upper \nlow across the central Conus. This is due to the Florida low \nlikely wrapping dry air from the northwest over our region \nsouthward into the western Atlantic. This is evident on forecast\ntotal PW values from both the ECMWF and GFS during this \ntimeframe. \n\nRegardless, it does look like the region will see some\nprecipitation and unsettled weather; however, the amount of\nprecipitation in question might just be much less than was\ninitially forecast earlier in the week. Overall, there is still\ntime for this forecast to change as it is still in the 5-7 day\ntimeframe, and with this many chess pieces on the board, there\nis certainly going to be more changes ahead of Thursday through\nSaturday of next week.",
